<section class="article-header"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/en/">International Clients</a> / Buying Property in Turkey</div><div class="tag">Real Estate Law · Foreign Property Buyers</div><h1>Buying Property in Turkey as a Foreigner: What Should You Check Before You Buy?</h1><p class="article-summary">Foreign buyers can purchase real estate in Turkey subject to statutory restrictions, but the title deed itself is only one part of the legal picture. A safe purchase requires checking ownership, encumbrances, zoning, permits, contractual terms and the buyer’s eligibility before money changes hands.</p><div class="article-meta">Published: 8 September 2026 · Ozan Ferhat Şen, Attorney at Law</div></div></section>
<section class="wrap article-layout"><article class="article-body">
<p>Buying an apartment, house, commercial unit or land in Turkey can look straightforward: agree on a price, sign documents and complete the transfer at the land registry. For a foreign buyer, however, the legal risk often sits behind the visible transaction.</p>
<p>The central question is not simply whether the seller has a title deed. The more useful question is whether the property can legally be acquired by that buyer, whether the title is burdened by rights or debts, whether the building is lawful, whether the intended use is possible and whether the contract protects the buyer if the transaction does not proceed as expected.</p>
<h2>1. First check whether the buyer is legally eligible to acquire the property</h2>
<p>Foreign natural persons may acquire real estate in Turkey only within the limits set by Turkish law. Article 35 of the Land Registry Law No. 2644 allows acquisition by nationals of countries determined by the competent Turkish authorities, subject to statutory restrictions.</p>
<p>For foreign natural persons, the total area acquired throughout Turkey may not exceed 30 hectares, and foreign ownership is also subject to a district-level limit of 10% of the area that may be held in private ownership. Certain military, security and strategic zones are excluded from foreign acquisition.</p>
<p>A residence permit is not, by itself, a general legal requirement for a foreign natural person to purchase property. Nationality and the location and legal status of the property are more important at the eligibility stage.</p>
<h2>2. Verify the seller and the title deed before paying a deposit</h2>
<p>The seller’s name should match the registered owner in the land registry. If a representative is acting for the seller, the scope and validity of the power of attorney should be reviewed carefully.</p>
<p>The title record should also be checked for mortgages, attachments, usufruct rights, easements, annotations, restrictions and other registered rights. A buyer should not assume that a clean-looking apartment or a seller’s assurance means that the title is legally unencumbered.</p>
<p>This review should take place before a significant deposit is paid or a binding agreement is signed.</p>
<h2>3. Do not treat the title deed as proof that the building is legally problem-free</h2>
<p>A registered independent unit may still raise planning and construction-law questions. Depending on the property, the review may need to include the zoning status, building permit, approved architectural project and occupancy permit.</p>
<p>These issues matter because the legal use of a property, future renovation, financing, redevelopment and resale value can all be affected by planning or permit problems.</p>
<h2>4. Check whether the property you are buying is the property you were shown</h2>
<p>Foreign buyers should make sure that the block, parcel, independent unit number and physical property correspond to each other. In practice, marketing materials, apartment numbers used by a developer and land-registry identifiers do not always speak the same language.</p>
<p>For land purchases, the cadastral parcel, boundaries, access, zoning designation and development rights deserve particular attention. For apartments, the registered independent unit and its approved use should be confirmed.</p>
<h2>5. If the property is unfinished or off-plan, contract risk becomes central</h2>
<p>When the purchase concerns a project under construction, legal review should go beyond the future title deed. The agreement should address the exact unit, completion date, specifications, payment schedule, delay, termination, refund rights and the consequences of changes to the project.</p>
<p>The buyer should also understand what security exists if the developer fails to complete the project. A glossy brochure is not a substitute for contractual protection.</p>
<h2>6. Be careful with reservation forms and deposits</h2>
<p>A document described as a “reservation”, “booking” or “deposit form” may still create financial consequences. Before paying, the buyer should know whether the payment is refundable, under what conditions it can be retained and what happens if legal due diligence reveals a problem.</p>
<p>A sensible structure is to make important payments dependent on satisfactory legal review where the transaction allows it.</p>
<h2>7. Review the power of attorney carefully if you are not completing the transaction personally</h2>
<p>Foreign buyers frequently use powers of attorney for property transactions in Turkey. The document should grant no broader authority than is necessary for the intended transaction.</p>
<p>Authority to purchase property is different from unrestricted authority to borrow, mortgage, sell, receive money or enter into unrelated transactions. The wording therefore matters.</p>
<h2>8. International buyers often describe the same problem in different languages</h2>
<p>A buyer in Germany may begin with a search such as <em>Immobilie in der Türkei kaufen</em>; a Russian-speaking buyer may search <span lang="ru">покупка недвижимости в Турции</span>; an Arabic-speaking buyer may search <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">شراء عقار في تركيا</span>. The language changes, but the legal questions are usually the same: can I buy, is the title clean, is the building lawful, what am I signing, and can the transaction be completed safely?</p>
<p>This page remains in English because the legal analysis is intended for international clients who are comfortable reading English, while the underlying search problem often begins in the buyer’s own language.</p>
<h2>9. Unbuilt land is subject to an additional rule</h2>
<p>According to the guidance of the General Directorate of Land Registry and Cadastre, a foreign natural person who acquires unbuilt real estate is required to develop a project for the property and submit it to the relevant ministry within two years from the acquisition date. The applicable ministry then determines the period for completion of the project.</p>
<p>This makes legal and planning due diligence particularly important for foreign buyers considering vacant land.</p>
<h2>10. Property ownership and Turkish citizenship are not the same legal question</h2>
<p>A foreigner may be legally able to buy a property without that purchase qualifying for Turkish citizenship. Citizenship by investment has its own conditions, procedural requirements and restrictions.</p>
<p>If citizenship is part of the investment objective, the transaction should be structured for that purpose from the beginning rather than treating citizenship as an automatic consequence of ownership.</p>
<h2>11. A practical pre-purchase legal checklist</h2>
<ul><li>Confirm that the buyer’s nationality is eligible for the intended acquisition.</li><li>Confirm that the property is not located in an area closed or restricted to foreign acquisition.</li><li>Verify the registered owner.</li><li>Review mortgages, attachments, annotations, usufruct rights, easements and other encumbrances.</li><li>Match the land-registry details with the physical property.</li><li>Review zoning status and development rights where relevant.</li><li>Check the building permit and occupancy permit for built property where relevant.</li><li>Review the sale, reservation or preliminary agreement before payment.</li><li>Check the scope of any power of attorney.</li><li>For off-plan property, review completion, delay, refund and security provisions.</li><li>For unbuilt land, consider the project obligation applicable to foreign buyers.</li><li>If citizenship is an objective, verify the separate citizenship requirements before structuring the purchase.</li></ul>
<h2>The safest time for legal review is before the transaction becomes difficult to reverse</h2>
<p>Many property disputes do not begin with an obviously fraudulent transaction. They begin with an unchecked mortgage, a permit problem, a mismatch between the promised unit and the registered unit, an overly broad power of attorney or a contract that does not say what happens when the project is delayed.</p>
<p>For that reason, legal due diligence is generally most useful before the buyer pays a substantial deposit, signs a binding agreement or gives a representative broad authority.</p>
